WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.930
It's great to welcome to the program today.
EJJ Deon, who is a columnist for the Washington

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:11.690
post senior fellow at the Brookings institution
and also author of code red, how progressives

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:17.200
and moderates can unite to save our country.
So I guess the first question is unite to

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.820
save the country from who or what. Right.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:27.830
Right. And, uh, in the first instance, it's
obviously Trump, Trump ism the attack on the

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:33.319
rule of law that we've been seeing a lot very
recently, but all through, uh, the administration

00:00:33.319 --> 00:00:39.270
to turn away from democracy, but it's also
to save the country from a radicalized right

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:46.089
and radicalized Republican party. Um, the
book is obviously a very anti Trump book,

00:00:46.089 --> 00:00:54.069
uh, but it is also very critical of a radical
right that really now dominates the Republican

00:00:54.069 --> 00:01:00.549
party and is driven out all vestiges of, uh,
both moderation. And back in the day, even

00:01:00.549 --> 00:01:06.520
some liberalism, uh, progressivism that existed
inside the Republican party. And so I already

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.880
knew that in this circumstance, uh, Progressive's
and moderates, and we can argue about the

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:16.990
term moderate, I'm, I'm careful in the book
to say, I know moderate itself is a vexed

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:23.350
term, but we couldn't say the center left
and the left, uh, as well, that they have

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:27.100
far more in common each other at this moment.

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:32.610
Uh, then, uh, they, um, then they have with
anyone on the other side, their differences

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:38.460
are much smaller than there are differences
of where the other side and a subsidiary argument

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:44.950
is that one of the reasons arguments inside
the democratic party can get so fierce is

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:51.009
that, um, people who are on the progressive
side have lost a sort of sensible interlocutor

00:01:51.009 --> 00:01:57.880
in the Republican party that almost all the
major conversations about solving public problems

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:04.460
take place inside the democratic party. So
Obamacare was a program that was built in

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:10.500
part on ideas developed by the heritage foundation
and by Mitt Romney in Massachusetts. And this

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:17.540
was cast as somehow socialist by the other
side. And so Democrats are now having the

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:23.540
argument that might in another era have taken
place across party lines. You expand on Obamacare

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:28.210
or do you go to single payer? That's the kind
of argument I make in the book, but I argue

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:35.830
to get past this, uh, moment, both of the
Trump moment and of a radicalized conservatism,

00:02:35.830 --> 00:02:42.220
Progressive's and moderates need to be allied
because that's the only way out of this, um,

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.060
this morass we're in.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:52.530
So does that presume this idea of vote for
the democratic nominee no matter who they

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:57.160
are? Like is that a related idea to what you
are arguing?

00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:03.560
Right. I think in this circumstance there
is pretty much no alternative to that and

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:09.220
it's obvious that there are, you know, a lot
of Democrats, especially after last night,

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:14.570
um, who might have second thoughts about voting
for Michael Bloomberg be my exact question

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:23.100
because, um, you know, and I think that, that,
that will be, um, more difficult. I mean,

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:27.450
I think it was difficult going into that debate.
I think it's even more difficult coming out

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:32.840
of that debate. Similarly, there are people
on the right end, if you will, of this broad

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:39.720
coalition I'm describing who have some questions
about Bernie Sanders at this moment. Um, uh,

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:44.510
I think that Bloomberg question will be the
harder question for an awful lot of people,

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:51.340
both on the left. Uh, and, um, you know, after
the demolition job that, uh, Elizabeth Warren

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.170
did, particularly among progressive women.

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:58.540
Yeah. And, uh, you know, I've said I'm not
supporting Bloomberg in the primary, but if

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:02.070
it's November 3rd, and it's Bloomberg versus
Trump, I'm voting for Bloomberg. And I've

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:07.520
pointed out, I think on Supreme court justice
selections, Bloomberg will make far better

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.960
choices than what Trump has evidenced. He
would do. I've listed a few things and it

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:16.320
doesn't mean I'm any great fan of him, but
I know a lot of people in my audience don't

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.510
feel that way at all. And my, my sort of follow
on question to this is I believe in and support

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:29.340
what we would a vigorous debate during the
primary about which ideas should reign Supreme,

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:35.340
so to speak. But I wonder is there a point
at which it becomes self-destructive in the

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.480
eventual general election?

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:43.290
Yeah, I am worried about that. I, I, you may
have noticed, I have a column up today, uh,

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:50.480
where, uh, and this was a, I wrote most of
it before the debate. Um, that the kind of,

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:57.840
uh, demolition Derby that Democrats are having,
um, is leading to, they're ignoring right

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:03.430
on that stage last night, what is going on
outside. It was really striking how little

00:05:03.430 --> 00:05:09.790
Trump himself was mentioned. We are really
at a potential constitutional crisis here

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:16.130
with what's going on inside the justice department.
What has gone on with Roger Stone, how all

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:22.600
these cases are being cherry picked, uh, on
Ukraine and Russia by, uh, by bar. These are

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:28.940
very disturbing things. It did disturb me
that Democrats were spending so much time,

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:35.920
um, sort of settling their own differences
that they didn't even get to. Um, Hey, we're

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:39.430
all here because we need to put a stop to
this.

00:05:39.430 --> 00:05:44.100
You know, on your lumber point. I think you're
correct that on court appointments, on what

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:50.040
his budgets would look like, even his tax
program is far more progressive than anything.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:55.490
Um, Republicans had to offer. And his support
for both the climate movements and especially

00:05:55.490 --> 00:06:00.980
the gun movements is a real thing. He's made
a huge difference there. Um, I think that

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:06.400
what we saw last night will raise questions
not only on the left, but a lot of other people

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:12.010
saying, is this ag, is he actually the strongest
candidate to put against Trump? I think that

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:17.260
was the argument he needed to make. And clearly
last night on that prompt was a fail.

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:22.490
No, certainly if, if his making billions dependent
on winning debates, I don't know that he would

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:29.470
be nearly nearly as wealthy as a, as he is
was, was my reaction. Yeah. So now in terms

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:36.150
of when we've seen in the past elections that
are heavily based around defeating the incumbent,

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:43.260
so John Kerry in 2004 against George W. Bush
strikes me as one of those. Um, there is often

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:50.060
the argument made that you've really gotta
be voting for something as opposed to trying

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:54.630
to remove the incumbent that it often doesn't
go well when the main message is removed,

00:06:54.630 --> 00:07:00.550
the incumbent. So do you agree with that and
is that maybe something that tempers the problem

00:07:00.550 --> 00:07:05.090
of, of Trump not being mentioned that much
yesterday? Which in a way I found refreshing

00:07:05.090 --> 00:07:08.670
to just hear the candidates talk about something
other than Trump.

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:14.310
Well, I think there are two sides to this
and maybe we can take Ronald Reagan as the

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:20.350
example. One of my arguments in the book by
the way, is that w uh, uh, a key reason why

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:27.210
there has a resurgence of democratic socialism
in the U S which by the way has a long American

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:31.690
tradition. As I point out in the book, Eugene
Debs got 6% of the boat. There were actually

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:37.670
1200 local elected officials who were socialists
back in that election. Um, the reason why

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:44.360
you've had a revival of a democratic socialism
is a reaction to the dominance of our public

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:50.580
conversation, um, by the Reagan economic consensus.
And so the rise of democratic socialism is,

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:56.090
uh, a reaction against that. But what did
Reagan do to create that long, had Gemini

00:07:56.090 --> 00:08:01.949
that long a period of conservative dominance?
I think there were two sides of it. I think

00:08:01.949 --> 00:08:07.760
running against an incumbent who's unpopular,
uh, is actually a helpful thing.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:12.860
Politically. Jimmy Carter was unpopular and
I argue in the book for what I call the power

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:18.620
of negative thinking. Uh, that when you looked
at what Ronald Reagan built his program on,

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:25.070
it started out as a series of negatives. Anti-tax
uh, anticommunist anti-government, I think

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:30.960
what posts together this coalition are a similar
set of antis obviously anti-Trump and all

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:37.139
the things that represents, uh, Yohei the
oppression of immigrants and refugees, a,

00:08:37.139 --> 00:08:43.930
you know, corporate corruption cast as populism.
Um, a whole disrespect for women, a whole

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:52.480
series of, uh, things. Um, but also a backlash
against, um, a Republican party that is simply

00:08:52.480 --> 00:08:58.639
given up on public problem solving, on using
government, uh, to redress inequality, uh,

00:08:58.639 --> 00:09:04.220
to provide health insurance a whole series
of areas where they propose simply retreating,

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:11.709
cutting taxes, relying entirely on the public
sector. But out of that negative comes a series

00:09:11.709 --> 00:09:13.620
of positives.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:19.319
And if I can just pick three quick issues
that are really burning issues that raise

00:09:19.319 --> 00:09:26.550
real coalition building possibilities. Um,
right now the entire democratic party, the

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:33.600
entire spectrum from center to left now wants
to provide affordable, good health insurance

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:39.430
to every American. That's not even debated
anymore. The debate is over whether we do

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:45.470
it through a public option in building on
Obamacare or go directly up to single pair.

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:51.889
Lord, I do not think we should divide ourselves
radically over the path. We can disagree,

00:09:51.889 --> 00:09:58.319
we can debate the path, but we all want to
go broadly in the same direction. AOC underscored

00:09:58.319 --> 00:10:04.399
that recently, um, with her comments. A second
one is free college. That's not so radical.

00:10:04.399 --> 00:10:09.470
We used to have it before the massive underinvestment
in public education. Whether we get there

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:16.209
in one step or many steps and let's make sure
this includes not only college of, but also

00:10:16.209 --> 00:10:18.790
training for people who don't want to go to
college.

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:23.449
I'm in the book, I quote Richard Cordray ran
for governor of Ohio. Unfortunately he didn't

00:10:23.449 --> 00:10:28.269
win, but I love this line. He said, you shouldn't
have to go to college to join the middle-class.

00:10:28.269 --> 00:10:34.009
I think we need to broaden that conversation,
but that's got a lot of possibility. And lastly,

00:10:34.009 --> 00:10:39.360
the green new deal, which when you boil the
green new deal down, it is saying that we

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:45.279
need urgently to act on the climate and we
also urgently need to restructure the economy

00:10:45.279 --> 00:10:51.050
to make it fairer. Uh, and we need to do that
partly to redress losses that some groups

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:57.199
might suffer from taking bold action on the
climate. I think these are all positives that

00:10:57.199 --> 00:11:03.860
grow out of all of those negatives. So I guess
I am not worried that a pure anti Trump campaign

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:09.420
will lack a positive vision. Uh, because I
think you're hearing a lot of positives in

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:15.129
all these, in the mess of all these debates,
there were actually a lot of proposals. Well,

00:11:15.129 --> 00:11:22.290
book a, I want to make clear is not proposing
this vague idea of unity for the sake of unity.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:26.899
The way that we've seen, uh, criticized over
the last couple of months as we've seen the

00:11:26.899 --> 00:11:35.920
democratic primary shift. It's really a practical
and a sensible perspective on what it is simply

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:42.070
that needs to happen. The book is by EJD on,
it is called code red. How Progressive's and

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:44.610
moderates can unite to save our [inaudible]

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:49.280
country. MJ, so great having you on. I appreciate
your time. Thank you so much. I hope we can

00:11:49.280 --> 00:11:49.300
do this again.

